{"name":"Cerignola Olive","hex":"#997b00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cerignola olive","blocks":[{"text":"Cerignola, also known as Bella di Cerignola or Cerignola Pendola, is an olive cultivar from Italy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Olive_di_Cerignola.jpg/330px-Olive_di_Cerignola.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerignola_olive","wikidata":"Q3637726"},{"title":"Cerignola","blocks":[{"text":"Cerignola is a town and comune (municipality) in the province of Foggia of the region of Apulia in Italy, 40 kilometres southeast from the town of Foggia. It has the third-largest land area of any municipality in Italy, at 593.93 square kilometres (229.32 sq mi), after Rome and Ravenna, and it has the largest land area of any municipality in Italy among those that are not the provincial capital. It has 56,711 inhabitants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Chiesa_Carmine_01.jpg/330px-Chiesa_Carmine_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerignola","wikidata":"Q19277"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Teatro_Mercadante_01.jpg/500px-Teatro_Mercadante_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Teatro_Mercadante_01.jpg/500px-Teatro_Mercadante_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mercadante Theatre.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeatro_Mercadante_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Palazzo_Pavoncelli.jpg/500px-Palazzo_Pavoncelli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Palazzo_Pavoncelli.jpg/500px-Palazzo_Pavoncelli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pavoncelli Palace.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalazzo_Pavoncelli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.